{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/gw3pzo","slideIndex":1,"text":"(0:30–1:15) The project addresses a familiar workflow problem. Developers often find a project through a search, then open several pages to compare maintenance signals, languages, popularity, and open issues. It is easy to lose track of promising candidates. This application gives that early research a single place. A user searches GitHub, refines the results by language, topic, or time period, and sorts by stars, forks, issues, or recent updates. From a result, the user can open a repository profile, bookmark it, and write a personal note. The intended outcome is a useful shortlist, not a replacement for a full code review."}
